  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Butternut canker is a fungal disease caused by Ophiognomonia clavigignenti-juglandacearum that severely damages and often kills butternut trees (Juglans cinerea) throughout New England. The pathogen creates expanding cankers on branches and trunks, eventually girdling and killing affected trees.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Elongated cankers with callus ridges",
      "description": "Look for dark, sunken lesions on branches and trunks with characteristic ridged callus tissue at the margins forming a distinctive pattern",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Branch dieback and flagging",
      "description": "Infected branches die back progressively, causing yellowing and wilting of foliage distal to the canker",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Blackish ooze and fruiting bodies",
      "description": "Dark, sticky ooze may exude from cankers, and small black perithecia (fungal fruiting structures) may be visible on the canker surface",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Butternut canker thrives in the moist climate and native butternut forests of New England, particularly affecting stressed or weakened trees in mixed hardwood ecosystems.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "This disease has devastated butternut populations so severely that butternut trees are now considered a species of special concern in many New England states, with some regions losing over 90% of their butternut trees to this pathogen since its arrival in the 1970s.",
